Title: SEVENTEEN MINUTES
Credit: Written by
Author: GREENLIGHT test fixture
Draft date: 2026-09-05
Notes: Version 2 du scénario de test, destinée à la démonstration du mode diff. Le bar et l'hôpital sont renommés, le numéro hors plage est corrigé, une scène est ajoutée, et le Chicago Tribune passe d'un accessoire neutre à l'instrument d'un délit. Tout le reste est inchangé : ces entités-là doivent voir leur verdict repris sans nouvelle recherche.

FADE IN:

INT. THE PAPER LANTERN - NIGHT

Low ceilings. A neon sign buzzes behind the bar, half its letters dead. Six stools, four of them empty.

DANIEL REYES, 34, sits alone with an untouched beer. He has been here long enough that the head has gone flat.

The door opens. Cold air. Daniel does not turn around.

A man slides onto the stool beside him. MARCUS WEBB, 50s, still in surgical scrubs under a good coat.

MARCUS
You're late.

DANIEL
I'm early. You're just impatient.

MARCUS
I've been on since six this morning.

DANIEL
Then you should have said seven.

Marcus sets a padded envelope on the bar. Slides it over with two fingers. Daniel doesn't touch it.

MARCUS
Forty. Same as last month.

DANIEL
It was thirty last month.

MARCUS
Prices went up. Ask the FDA.

DANIEL
The FDA doesn't set your prices.

MARCUS
No. It sets how hard it is to get, which is the same thing wearing a nicer coat.

Daniel finally takes the envelope. Weighs it. Slips it inside his jacket without opening it.

DANIEL
If anyone traces this back to the hospital —

MARCUS
Nobody traces anything. I sign the charts. I sign the counts. I sign the disposal log at the end of every shift, and the disposal log says what I say it says.

DANIEL
That's not an answer.

MARCUS
It's the only answer there is.

He stands. Buttons the coat over the scrubs. Pauses.

MARCUS (CONT'D)
Seventeen minutes.

DANIEL
What?

MARCUS
That's how long a cabinet stays unlocked between the count and the seal. Seventeen minutes, every night, in every hospital in this city. Nobody has ever asked me why.

He leaves. The door swings twice and settles.

Daniel looks at the beer. Still doesn't drink it.

INT. SAINT ODILE MEDICAL CENTER - EMERGENCY ROOM - CONTINUOUS

Fluorescent hum. A CLERK works a triage desk under a wall clock that reads 11:48.

Marcus pushes through the double doors, coat off, badge already clipped to his scrubs.

A NURSE intercepts him with a chart.

NURSE
Bay four's asking for you. Again.

MARCUS
Tell bay four I'm a doctor, not a vending machine.

NURSE
He's been here since two.

MARCUS
Then he's had nine hours to get better on his own.

He takes the chart without slowing. Scans it. Signs the bottom without reading the middle.

NURSE
Doctor Webb.

He stops.

NURSE (CONT'D)
Pharmacy called. The count's off again.

A beat. Marcus's face does not change.

MARCUS
By how much?

NURSE
They didn't say. They said to tell you.

MARCUS
Then they'll say it to me in the morning, when I'm a person and not a shift.

He walks. The nurse watches him go, one second longer than she needs to.

EXT. HOSPITAL PARKING STRUCTURE - LEVEL 3 - NIGHT

Concrete. Sodium light. Half the bays empty.

Daniel leans against a grey sedan, plate 7XKD429. He dials a number on a burner phone.

DANIEL (V.O.)
It's done. He took it.

The line clicks dead before he finishes. He checks the screen: 555-0147.

He dials a second number from memory: 312-555-0190. It rings once. He hangs up.

DANIEL (CONT'D)
Wrong one.

He drops the burner into a storm drain. It doesn't make a sound.

A blue FORD EXPLORER rolls past the end of the row and does not stop. Daniel watches it until the tail lights turn.

INT. WALGREENS - PHARMACY COUNTER - NIGHT

Bright. Empty. A radio somewhere plays something with no words.

Daniel slides a paper script across the counter. The PHARMACIST, 20s, takes it without looking up.

PHARMACIST
Insurance?

DANIEL
Cash.

That gets a look. The pharmacist reads the script properly now. Oxycontin, eighty milligrams, quantity ninety.

PHARMACIST
This is a lot.

DANIEL
It's what he wrote.

PHARMACIST
Doctor —

(reading)

— Webb. Saint Odile.

DANIEL
That's him.

The pharmacist types. Waits. Types again. The screen takes its time.

PHARMACIST
I have to call it in.

DANIEL
Go ahead.

He doesn't move. Doesn't blink. The pharmacist picks up the phone, dials four digits, and lets it ring twice before hanging up.

PHARMACIST
Line's busy.

DANIEL
It's midnight.

PHARMACIST
Yeah.

She fills it. Staples the bag. Slides it over.

PHARMACIST (CONT'D)
Have a good night.

DANIEL
You too.

He leaves. She watches the door for a while after it closes, then writes something on a sticky note and puts it in her pocket.

INT. DANIEL'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Bare walls. A folding table. A laptop, a lamp, and nothing else that suggests anyone lives here.

Above the desk, a print of Nighthawks, curling at one corner, held with a single pin.

Daniel logs into a webmail client. The address bar reads dreyes@example.com.

He types one line, deletes it, types it again.

ON SCREEN
"Seventeen minutes. That's all it took."

He does not send it. He saves it to drafts, closes the laptop, and sits in the dark.

A Coca-Cola can sweats on the windowsill beside him.

On the shelf below: a paperback by Studs Terkel, spine broken, and a Chicago Cubs cap that has clearly never been worn to a game.

INT. SAINT ODILE MEDICAL CENTER - RECORDS ROOM - DAY

Rows of terminals. Nobody here but Marcus and a hum.

He logs in. Pulls a patient list. Sorts by discharge date.

He opens a chart. Changes one number. Saves. Opens the next.

A DOOR opens behind him. He does not turn.

CLERK (O.S.)
You're not supposed to be in here alone.

MARCUS
I'm not alone. You're here.

CLERK
That's not how the policy reads.

MARCUS
Then the policy should have said so on the door.

The clerk waits. Marcus keeps working. After a moment the door closes again.

He opens the next chart. Changes one number. Saves.

A terminal two rows over wakes up on its own. Somebody, somewhere, has run a query.

Marcus watches the screen do nothing for eleven seconds. Then he goes back to work.

He opens a chart from fourteen months ago. Reads it properly, for once. A woman's name, an admission date, a discharge that never happened.

He does not change this one. He closes it and sits with his hands on the desk.

MARCUS (V.O.)
Nobody subtracts. That's the whole thing. Everybody in this building can add — add a dose, add a shift, add a bed — and not one of them has ever sat down with two columns and taken one away from the other.

He logs out. The screen asks whether he is sure. He is.

EXT. 1060 WEST ADDISON STREET - DAY

Under the marquee. Game day traffic, a mounted cop, a man selling ponchos out of a duffel bag.

Daniel waits with his hands in his pockets. He is wearing the Cubs cap now.

ELENA VARGAS, 40s, arrives with two coffees and hands him one without asking.

ELENA
You picked a stupid place to meet.

DANIEL
Forty thousand people. Nobody looks at anybody.

ELENA
Forty thousand people and about nine hundred cameras.

DANIEL
Pointed at the field.

ELENA
Pointed at everything.

She drinks. Waits. He doesn't start.

ELENA (CONT'D)
You called me.

DANIEL
I know.

ELENA
So call me.

He takes an envelope out of his jacket. Not the padded one — a plain one, letter size.

DANIEL
Disposal logs. Fourteen months. Every one of them signed by the same hand.

ELENA
That's not a story. That's paperwork.

DANIEL
The counts don't match the logs. Not once. Not in fourteen months.

Elena stops drinking.

ELENA
Where did you get this?

DANIEL
Does it matter?

ELENA
To me, no. To the Reader's lawyers, enormously.

INT. THE PAPER LANTERN - LATER

The bar is empty now. A radio behind the counter plays "Sweet Child O' Mine", tinny and low.

The BARTENDER wipes down the counter, watching the door.

Daniel enters. Sits in the same seat. Same untouched beer, or one exactly like it.

BARTENDER
He's not coming back, you know.

DANIEL
He always comes back.

BARTENDER
Not after tonight.

DANIEL
You don't know him.

BARTENDER
I know that chair. Thirty years of it.

She slides a folded copy of the Chicago Tribune across the bar. The front page carries a forged byline Daniel planted himself, paid for in cash to a stringer who never checked.

He reads his own lie twice. Then he folds it, very carefully, along the crease it already had.

DANIEL
When did this come out?

BARTENDER
This morning.

DANIEL
And you waited until now.

BARTENDER
You came in at eleven. I gave it to you at eleven.

INT. FORD EXPLORER (MOVING) - NIGHT

Daniel drives. Wipers on the slow setting. The city goes by in wet orange.

In the passenger seat, a kid — TOMÁS, 19, in a Blackhawks jersey two sizes too big — holds the pharmacy bag in his lap like it's warm.

TOMÁS
How much do we get?

DANIEL
We don't get anything. You get two hundred.

TOMÁS
That's not what Ray said.

DANIEL
Ray isn't driving.

Silence. The wipers.

TOMÁS
My mom worked at Saint Odile. Housekeeping. Eleven years.

DANIEL
I didn't ask.

TOMÁS
I'm just saying I know the building.

DANIEL
That's exactly why you're in this car and not in that building.

Tomás looks out the window. The jersey has somebody's number on the back, and it isn't his.

INT. CHICAGO READER - ELENA'S OFFICE - DAY

Glass. Open plan. Somebody's dog asleep under a desk.

Elena has the disposal logs spread across two monitors and half the floor.

A LAWYER, 50s, stands in the doorway with his coat still on.

LAWYER
Fourteen months of internal documents from a source you won't name.

ELENA
The documents name themselves. Every page has a signature on it.

LAWYER
A signature you can't authenticate.

ELENA
I can authenticate the counts. The counts are public. The state files them.

LAWYER
That is not the part that worries me.

ELENA
The last time this paper was sued properly it was over the 1968 Democratic National Convention, and we were right then too.

LAWYER
Then run the counts and leave the hospital's name out of it.

ELENA
The hospital's name is the story.

LAWYER
The hospital's name is a lawsuit.

Elena picks up a page. Holds it up. Does not hand it to him.

ELENA
Seventeen minutes. Every night. In every hospital in this city, according to the man who wrote this. If that's true, this isn't one hospital, it's a hole in the floor.

LAWYER
Then find me two hospitals.

INT. SAINT ODILE MEDICAL CENTER - STAIRWELL - NIGHT

Concrete and green paint. The door two floors up bangs and echoes.

Marcus comes down fast. Daniel is waiting on the landing.

MARCUS
You don't come here.

DANIEL
The counts are filed with the state.

MARCUS
Everything's filed with the state.

DANIEL
Fourteen months, Marcus. Anybody who subtracts can see it.

Marcus stops on the step above him. Uses the height.

MARCUS
Nobody subtracts.

DANIEL
Somebody did.

A long beat. Marcus reads his face and gets there.

MARCUS
What did you give her?

DANIEL
Paper. That's all. Paper you left in a bin.

MARCUS
I shredded that bin.

DANIEL
On Tuesdays.

Marcus laughs, once, with nothing in it.

MARCUS
Do you know what happens to you in this? Nothing. You're a name in a paragraph. I'm a photograph.

DANIEL
Then stop.

MARCUS
It's too late to stop. It was too late the first night, I just didn't know it for another four hundred.

EXT. 4400 NORTH BROADWAY - DAWN

Grey light. A shuttered storefront with paper in the windows.

Daniel stands across the street, hands in his pockets, no cap.

A CPD cruiser rolls past without slowing. He doesn't watch it go.

He waits until it turns the corner. Then he crosses.

INT. CPD DISTRICT 19 - INTERVIEW ROOM - DAY

Two chairs, a table bolted down, a camera in the corner with a live red light.

DETECTIVE SHAW, 40s, sits across from Daniel with a folder she hasn't opened.

SHAW
You came in on your own.

DANIEL
I did.

SHAW
Nobody does that.

DANIEL
Then this'll be a short morning for both of us.

She opens the folder. Turns a page around. It's the Tribune front page, the same one, in an evidence sleeve.

SHAW
You knew him.

DANIEL
I knew him.

SHAW
How.

DANIEL
He was my sister's doctor. Then he wasn't. Then he was something else.

SHAW
That's three answers and none of them are how.

Daniel looks at the camera. Then back at her.

DANIEL
Seventeen minutes. Ask him what that means. He'll tell you, he can't help it, it's the only interesting thing he's ever done.

SHAW
He's not going to tell me anything.

DANIEL
Why not?

She turns another page around. Daniel reads it. Doesn't move.

A long time passes. The camera's red light does not blink.

SHAW (CONT'D)
Tuesday night. Where were you.

DANIEL
Driving.

SHAW
Where.

DANIEL
Broadway. North, then south, then north again. I wasn't going anywhere, I was just going.

SHAW
Anyone with you?

DANIEL
A kid.

SHAW
Name.

DANIEL
He's nineteen and he carried a bag he didn't open. That's the whole of what he did.

SHAW
That's not a name.

DANIEL
No. It isn't.

Shaw writes something. Underlines it.

SHAW
You understand that I can't help you if you decide which parts of this are mine.

DANIEL
I'm not asking for help.

SHAW
Then what are you asking for?

Daniel thinks about it longer than she expects.

DANIEL
For somebody to subtract.

SHAW
Meaning what?

DANIEL
The counts are filed with the state. Fourteen months of them. Nobody in this city has ever put the two columns next to each other, and the whole thing has been sitting in a public database the entire time, waiting for somebody to be bored enough.

Shaw closes the folder.

SHAW
And you were bored enough.

DANIEL
My sister was.

He looks at the camera again. This time he keeps looking at it.

INT. RIVERTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE - CORRIDOR - DAY

Marble and bad acoustics. Daniel sits on a bench outside courtroom 4B, the plain envelope on his knees.

Across the corridor, ELENA waits with a photographer from the Chicago Reader and does not look at him.

A CLERK opens the door and reads a name off a sheet. It isn't his.

CLERK
Last call.

Nobody stands. The clerk goes back in.

Daniel turns the envelope over. On the back, in his own handwriting: an address, 4400 North Broadway, and a time.

He puts it in the bin by the door and walks out the other way.

INT. THE PAPER LANTERN - NIGHT

Later. Weeks later, by the light.

The neon sign has been fixed. All the letters work now, which somehow makes the place look worse.

The BARTENDER pours a beer nobody ordered and sets it on the bar in front of an empty stool.

On the radio, low: "Amazing Grace", sung slow by somebody who means it.

Daniel comes in. Sees the beer. Sits one stool over.

BARTENDER
That one's not for you.

DANIEL
I know.

They listen to the radio for a while.

BARTENDER
Did you say anything? At the thing?

DANIEL
No.

BARTENDER
Good.

She goes back to the counter. Daniel sits with the empty stool between them.

FADE OUT.
